// GENERATED by scripts/build-courses.ts from src/lib/courses/content — edit those files, then run: npm run courses
import type { LessonText } from '../types';
const text: LessonText[][] = [[{"say":"Economics is about making choices when you can't have everything. Let's learn to think like an economist.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"Scarcity means resources are limited but wants are unlimited, so every choice involves a trade-off. The opportunity cost of a choice is the value of the next best option you give up."},{"t":"p","text":"The factors of production are land (natural resources), labor (human effort), capital (tools and machines) and entrepreneurship (organizing the others to start businesses)."},{"t":"ex","problem":"You can work a shift for $60 or go to a concert that costs $40. What's the opportunity cost of the concert?","steps":["You pay $40 and give up $60 in wages"],"answer":"$100 total (the ticket plus the lost wages)"},{"t":"p","text":"Economists think at the margin: they compare the extra benefit of one more unit with its extra cost."}]},{"say":"Every society must answer three questions: what to produce, how to produce it, and for whom. Systems answer differently.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"Economic systems: traditional (based on customs), command (government decides), market (individuals and businesses decide through prices), and mixed (combines market and government roles). Most countries, including the US, have mixed economies."},{"t":"p","text":"In a market economy, prices act as signals: high prices encourage producers to make more and consumers to buy less. Competition and the profit motive drive efficiency and innovation."},{"t":"p","text":"The circular flow model shows households selling resources to businesses and buying their goods and services, with money flowing the other way."}]}],[{"say":"Supply and demand explain the price of almost everything you buy.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"Law of demand: as price rises, quantity demanded falls. Law of supply: as price rises, quantity supplied rises."},{"t":"p","text":"Demand curves shift when income, tastes, prices of related goods (substitutes and complements), expectations or the number of buyers change. Supply curves shift when input costs, technology, taxes or the number of sellers change."},{"t":"p","text":"A change in price causes a movement along the curve (change in quantity demanded); a change in another factor shifts the whole curve (change in demand)."},{"t":"ex","problem":"The price of hot dogs falls. What happens to demand for hot dog buns?","steps":["Hot dogs and buns are complements"],"answer":"Demand for buns increases"}]},{"say":"Where supply meets demand, the market finds a price that clears the market.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"Equilibrium is the price where quantity supplied equals quantity demanded. Above it, there's a surplus, and prices fall. Below it, there's a shortage, and prices rise."},{"t":"p","text":"Governments sometimes set prices: a price ceiling (maximum, like rent control) causes shortages if set below equilibrium; a price floor (minimum, like the minimum wage) causes surpluses if set above equilibrium."},{"t":"p","text":"Elasticity measures how much quantity responds to price. Necessities like medicine have inelastic demand; luxuries and goods with many substitutes have elastic demand."}]}],[{"say":"Personal finance skills help you make smart money decisions for life. It starts with a budget.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"A budget tracks income and expenses. The 50/30/20 guideline suggests about 50% for needs, 30% for wants and 20% for savings and debt payments."},{"t":"p","text":"Gross pay is what you earn before taxes; net pay is what you take home after deductions like income tax and Social Security."},{"t":"p","text":"Saving builds an emergency fund (often 3 to 6 months of expenses). Compound interest grows savings over time, because you earn interest on your interest."},{"t":"ex","problem":"You deposit $1,000 at 5% interest compounded yearly. How much after 2 years?","steps":["Year 1: 1,000 × 1.05 = 1,050","Year 2: 1,050 × 1.05"],"answer":"$1,102.50"}]},{"say":"Credit can help you or hurt you. Investing helps your money grow. Let's learn both.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"Credit lets you borrow money now and repay later with interest. A credit score (roughly 300 to 850) reflects how reliably you pay bills; it affects loans, apartments and sometimes jobs."},{"t":"p","text":"Credit cards charge high interest if you don't pay the full balance. Paying only the minimum can make debt last for years."},{"t":"p","text":"Investing options:"},{"t":"list","items":["Savings accounts: very safe, low returns.","Bonds: loans to governments or companies; moderate risk.","Stocks: ownership in companies; higher risk and higher potential returns.","Index funds: diversified collections of stocks with low fees."]},{"t":"p","text":"Diversification (spreading money across many investments) reduces risk."}]}],[{"say":"Macroeconomics looks at the whole economy. Three numbers tell us how it's doing.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"GDP (gross domestic product) is the total value of final goods and services produced in a country in a year. Growing real GDP means a growing economy."},{"t":"p","text":"The business cycle has expansions, peaks, contractions (recessions) and troughs."},{"t":"p","text":"Unemployment rate is the percent of the labor force looking for work but without a job. Inflation is a general rise in prices, measured by the Consumer Price Index (CPI). Inflation reduces purchasing power."},{"t":"ex","problem":"Prices rise 3% this year, but your wages rise only 1%. What happens to your purchasing power?","steps":["Prices grow faster than income"],"answer":"Your purchasing power falls"}]},{"say":"Governments and central banks use tools to smooth out the business cycle.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"Fiscal policy is government spending and taxes, set by Congress and the president. During a recession, the government may increase spending or cut taxes to boost demand."},{"t":"p","text":"Monetary policy is controlled by the Federal Reserve (the Fed). To fight a recession, it lowers interest rates and increases the money supply; to fight inflation, it raises interest rates."},{"t":"p","text":"Government spending more than it collects creates a budget deficit, which adds to the national debt."},{"t":"p","text":"Taxes can be progressive (higher rates for higher incomes), regressive (taking a larger share from lower incomes) or proportional (the same rate for all)."}]}],[{"say":"Countries trade because they can each produce some things more efficiently than others.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"Comparative advantage means producing a good at a lower opportunity cost than others. Countries benefit by specializing in what they do best and trading."},{"t":"p","text":"Exports are goods sold to other countries; imports are goods bought from them. A trade deficit means a country imports more than it exports."},{"t":"p","text":"Trade barriers include tariffs (taxes on imports), quotas (limits on amounts) and embargoes (bans). They protect some domestic industries but raise prices for consumers and can lead to trade wars."},{"t":"p","text":"Free trade agreements, like the USMCA between the US, Mexico and Canada, reduce barriers."}]},{"say":"The world's economies are more connected than ever. That brings opportunities and challenges.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"Globalization is the increasing connection of economies through trade, investment, technology and migration. It lowers prices and spreads innovation, but can move jobs overseas and widen inequality in some places."},{"t":"p","text":"Exchange rates are the price of one currency in another. A strong dollar makes imports cheaper for Americans but US exports more expensive for foreigners."},{"t":"p","text":"Developing countries can grow through trade, investment and education. International organizations like the World Trade Organization, the World Bank and the International Monetary Fund support trade and development."}]}]];
export default text;
